WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.000
Today you will build a small workflow: a form collects answers, AI finds the themes, and a script emails you the summary.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.920
Google Forms and Microsoft Forms collect answers in the same shape every time.

3
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.310
The answers go straight into a sheet, so nobody retypes anything.

4
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.530
Turn off collecting email addresses unless you really need them.

5
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:32.320
Remove any personal details, then paste the comments and ask for themes with counts and exact quotes.

6
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.650
Tell the AI to quote word for word and not to invent anything.

7
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:41.710
Then check the biggest claims yourself with COUNTIF or a filter.

8
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:47.320
Apps Script is a helper built into Google Sheets that runs small programs.

9
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.090
The AI can write a script that reads the responses and emails you a summary.

10
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:58.580
You do not need to be a programmer, but you must understand every line before you run it.

11
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.350
Before running any script, ask the AI to explain it line by line.

12
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:10.250
Make sure it only reads data, emails only you, and sends one email, not one for every row.

13
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.930
Test it on a copy of the sheet first, and know how to switch it off.

14
00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:20.120
A trigger runs your script automatically, for example every Monday morning.

15
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:26.270
With Microsoft Forms, Power Automate can do a similar job, and Zapier works with both.

16
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:31.960
They have free plans, but check the current free plan and your company rules first.

17
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.510
A form collects every answer in the same shape, which makes summarising easy.

18
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:43.390
Keep email collection off for honest, anonymous feedback, unless you truly need it.

19
00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:49.310
Link the form to a sheet, and every new answer lands in a new row by itself.

20
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:55.070
For practice, we use eighteen invented responses from a workshop feedback form.

21
00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:01.770
First we deleted a phone number that one person typed into a comment, then we pasted the responses.

22
00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:08.520
Claude groups the comments into four themes, with a count and an exact quote for each.

23
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.290
But the first theme says over half of the people found it too fast.

24
00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:20.350
A quick COUNTIF finds only five comments out of eighteen, so we correct the summary before sharing.

25
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:25.560
Now we ask ChatGPT for a small script that emails us one summary.

26
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.120
The script reads the rows, adds up the ratings, and counts the low ones.

27
00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.100
But look where the email line sits: inside the loop that visits every row.

28
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:41.860
That would send eighteen emails, so we move it after the loop, where it runs only once.

29
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.220
Here is the checked script, and the comments explain each part in plain words.

30
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.270
It opens the responses tab, reads every row, and skips the heading row.

31
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:56.480
The loop adds up the ratings and counts how many were one or two.

32
00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.790
After the loop, it works out the average and sends exactly one email, only to you.

33
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.740
It never changes or deletes anything, and that is the first thing you check.

34
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:13.880
Always practise on a copy, so a mistake can never touch the real responses.

35
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:19.870
The first time, Google asks for permission, so read exactly what the script wants to do.

36
00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.550
Check your inbox for one email with the right numbers.

37
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:29.740
Only then add a weekly trigger, and note how to delete it if you ever need to stop it.

38
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.630
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

39
00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:38.450
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
